<script>
	import Statement from '$lib/components/Statement.svelte';
	import Proof from '$lib/components/Proof.svelte';
	import * as declaration from './statements/declaration.md';
	import * as coreTypes from './statements/core-types.md';
	import * as area from './statements/area.md';
</script>

## The problem

A density is a density with respect to a measure. [The core](../core/math.svx) fixes one for each
of its own types, Lebesgue measure on the Euclidean and positive types, counting measure on the
discrete ones and product measures on products, and states every density, importance weight and
retirement against them ([the appendix](../core/math.svx#appendix-the-probability-space-of-a-model)).
It leaves the embedded types, the curved types that later crates declare, without one: their
points are stored in a coordinate space $\R^n$ of which they fill no open set, so Lebesgue measure
gives them none, and the appendix defers "surface measure on a sphere, Haar measure on the
rotations" to the entries that bring those types.

Every distribution with a density on a curved type needs that measure, and the first of them is
already being worked out: the normal distribution, generalized to the law of $\mu \oplus
\varepsilon$ for a normal $\varepsilon$ on any space whose chart's volume is known, so that a
measurement of a rotation or of a direction is held as what was measured rather than as a residual.
Its log-density is that of $\varepsilon$ less the log-volume of the chart at $\mu$
([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward)), and so it needs, of every type it is used on, a
reference measure and that log-volume as something it can compute and differentiate.

Asking each distribution's entry to supply them would put a type's measure outside the type, and
have every curved type answer the same question once per distribution. This entry answers it once,
for every type: it gives each continuous type of the core a reference measure and the log-volume
of its retraction's chart, lets an embedded type declare its own, and completes the appendix with
the rule for a type that declares none.

## Setting

The mathematics of densities in tangent charts is in
[the note on densities in tangent charts](../../math/chart-volume/), and this document uses its
notation throughout. A continuous type is a manifold $\manifold$ of dimension $\dimension$ with a
[retraction](../../math/retraction/) $\mu \oplus \tangent = R_\mu(\tangent)$; with a reference
measure $\nu$ on $\manifold$, the chart at $\mu$ has a domain $U_\mu \ni 0$ on which it is a
diffeomorphism onto its image $V_\mu$, and a volume $v_\mu$ on $U_\mu$, with
$\nu(R_\mu(A)) = \int_A v_\mu$ ([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/chart-volume)). Its log-volume
is $\lambda_\mu = \log v_\mu$. The chart is *global* if $U_\mu = \R^\dimension$, and *full* if
$V_\mu$ has a complement of measure zero. A random vector $\varepsilon \in U_\mu$ of density $f$
moves $\mu$ to a point of density $f(\point \ominus \mu) / v_\mu(\point \ominus \mu)$
([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward)).

An embedded type is declared to the core by its storage size $n$, its dimension $\dimension$, a
retraction $R : \R^n \times \R^\dimension \to \R^n$, its inverse $Q : \R^n \times \R^n \to
\R^\dimension$ and optionally a projection, each an operation on Euclidean values, smooth as
computed at $\tangent = 0$ and on the diagonal, with bases $B(\point) = \partial_2 R(\point, 0)$
([%full](statement:archive/core/math/embedded)). Its boundary operations are the inclusion
$\iota$, the retraction and the projection, whose derivative maps the core assembles from passes
over $R$, $Q$ and the projection. Every operation has derivative maps of first and second order at
most, tangent, cotangent and Hessian passes, and an order that says which of them it admits
([Operations](../core/math.svx#operations)).

The measure a retraction *normalizes* is the one whose charts have volume $1$ at their centre,
$v_\mu(0) = 1$ at every $\mu$. It exists on every manifold with a retraction, and is the
Riemannian volume of the metric in which every basis of the retraction is orthonormal, so that it
depends on the bases alone and not on how the manifold is stored
([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/basis)); for an embedded type it is
$\det(B\transpose B)^{-1/2}$ times surface measure ([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/normalized)).

## The core's own types

The core's types keep the measures the appendix gives them, and their charts' volumes follow
directly from their retractions.

<Statement {...coreTypes} />

<Proof>

On $\R^n$ the chart at $\mu$ is the translation $\tangent \mapsto \mu + \tangent$, a diffeomorphism
of $\R^n$ onto itself with Jacobian determinant $1$, so it is global and full with $v_\mu = 1$. A
matrix of $\R^{m \times n}$ is flattened column by column into $\R^{mn}$, which carries Lebesgue
measure to Lebesgue measure, so the same holds. On $\positive^n$, $R_\mu(\tangent) = \mu \odot
e^\tangent$ maps $\R^n$ onto $\positive^n$, with inverse $\log \point - \log \mu$, and its Jacobian
is $\operatorname{diag}(\mu \odot e^\tangent)$, whose determinant is
$\prod_i \mu_i e^{\tangent_i} = \prod_i (\mu \oplus \tangent)_i$; the change of variables formula
gives the volume. Products are [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/product).

</Proof>

As operations of the base point's storage and the tangent vector, the log-volumes are

$$
	\Lambda_{\R}(\mu, \tangent) = 0, \qquad
	\Lambda_{\positive}(\mu, \tangent) = \sum_{i=1}^n \bigl(\log \mu_i + \tangent_i\bigr), @tag(core-volumes)
$$

and read at the coordinate $\tangent = \point \ominus \mu$ of a point, the positive one is
$\Lambda_{\positive}(\mu, \point \ominus \mu) = \sum_i \log \point_i$, which does not depend on
$\mu$. With [](eq:core-volumes), [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward) gives back the
core's own formula for a density written in the logarithmic chart of a positive quantity,
[](eq:archive/core/math/log-chart-density): a vector whose logarithm has density $g$ has the
density $g(\log \point) \prod_i \point_i^{-1}$, which is $f(\point \ominus \mu) /
v_\mu(\point \ominus \mu)$ with $f(\tangent) = g(\log \mu + \tangent)$, at every $\mu$.

**Why the positive orthant keeps Lebesgue measure.** The measure the positive orthant's retraction
normalizes is not Lebesgue measure but $\prod_i \dd \point_i / \point_i$
([the note's example](../../math/chart-volume/#the-measure-a-retraction-normalizes)), so the core's
positive type is the one continuous type whose reference measure is not the one its retraction
normalizes, and the one whose log-volume depends on the base point. It keeps Lebesgue measure all
the same. The densities of positive quantities in common use, the gamma, the inverse gamma, the
log-normal and the exponential, are published and computed against Lebesgue measure, and a
log-density that differed from theirs by $\sum_i \log \point_i$ would surprise everyone comparing
skoro with them. The difference is not a constant: a mode with respect to $\dd \point / \point$ is a
mode in the logarithmic chart, and is not the mode a user of the gamma distribution expects. And the
appendix already fixes Lebesgue measure, which the core's tests rely on. The cost is that the
log-volume of a type must be allowed to depend on the base point, which an embedded type whose
bases are not of constant volume needs anyway.

## What an embedded type declares

An embedded type has no Lebesgue measure, and the surface measure its storage induces depends on
the storage: the rotations stored as matrices and as quaternions would have densities that differ
by a constant, and a type whose basis is not orthonormal, densities that differ by a function of
the point. The measure an embedded type is given is therefore the one its retraction normalizes.
On a group whose retraction translates a chart of the identity it is a Haar measure
([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/group)), and on a sphere with orthonormal bases it is surface
measure; on any type, it is the same whatever the storage, so long as the bases are, and a
density on the type means the same whether its points are stored as matrices or as quaternions.

The core cannot compute the chart's volume from a declared type in general (below), and the
appendix cannot give a measure to a type whose volume it cannot compute. So the chart's volume
becomes an optional declaration of an embedded type, beside its retraction, its inverse and its
projection.

<Statement {...declaration} />

The log-volume of the chart at $\mu$ is then the operation read at the base point's storage,

$$
	\lambda_\mu(\tangent) = \Lambda\bigl(\iota(\mu), \tangent\bigr), @tag(declared)
$$

a composite of the inclusion and an operation on Euclidean values, whose derivatives with respect
to $\mu$, in its chart, and to $\tangent$ follow by the chain rule from those of $\iota$
[](eq:archive/core/math/inclusion) and of $\Lambda$. On a group, $\Lambda$ does not depend on its
first argument ([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/group)), and neither does it for the
stereographic chart of a sphere with orthonormal bases, whose volume depends on the length of
$\tangent$ alone. In general it may, and it does on a type whose bases have a volume
$\det(B\transpose B)$ that varies from point to point.

Each condition of the definition is there for a use.

- **The inverse on the whole chart.** [%full](statement:archive/core/math/embedded) asks of $Q$
  only that it invert the retraction near $\tangent = 0$, which is enough for derivatives. A
  density at a point $\point$ is read at its coordinate $\point \ominus \mu$, which may be anywhere
  in the chart, so a type that declares a volume asks more: $Q(\mu, \cdot)$ is the inverse of the
  chart on its whole image. The usual charts have it: the logarithm of the rotations by less than
  $\pi$, the Cayley inverse [](eq:math/chart-volume/cayley-inverse), and the stereographic inverse
  [](eq:archive/core/math/sphere) away from $-\mu$.
- **The domain.** Within $U_\mu$, [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward) applies to
  every density of $\varepsilon$ whose mass is in $U_\mu$. A global chart takes every density on
  $\R^\dimension$, and a sampler that draws $\mu \oplus \varepsilon$ is then exact for the density
  [](eq:chart-density) below, whatever $\varepsilon$'s law. A chart that is not global, such as an
  exponential chart, is a diffeomorphism only on its domain, and the law of $\mu \oplus
  \varepsilon$ for an $\varepsilon$ with mass outside it is within that mass of the law the
  density describes ([%full](statement:math/chart-volume/local-chart)). Which of the two a type's
  chart is, and for a chart that is not global its domain, is therefore part of what a distribution
  must know to say whether its sampler is exact. The domain is declared as a discretization, an
  operation the core already has, whose output is a boolean and which has no derivatives, so that
  a test or a sampler can ask of a tangent vector whether it lies in the domain.
- **Fullness.** The image of a full chart is all of $\manifold$ but a set of measure zero, so a
  density in the chart is a density on $\manifold$, read correctly at every point that has
  positive probability. The complement may be where $Q$ is undefined, as $-\mu$ is for the
  stereographic chart, and is never reached by a draw. A chart that is not full, such as the
  normalization chart of the sphere, which reaches the open hemisphere around $\mu$ alone, defines
  a density that is zero outside its image, and its $Q$ computes a coordinate there all the same,
  at which a log-density is finite and wrong. The definition asks for fullness, so that no type's
  density can be silently wrong at a point a model can be given.
- **The log-volume as an operation.** $\Lambda$ is one more operation on Euclidean values, written
  as a composite or as a fused operation, and tested like one. A term reads it at $\tangent =
  \point \ominus \mu$, which is $0$ whenever the point is at its mean, as a measurement is when it
  equals its prediction; so it is asked to be smooth as computed there, as $R$ is
  ([Smooth as computed](../core/math.svx#quantities)). The exponential chart of the rotations has
  $\lambda = 2 \log \operatorname{sinc}(\theta / 2)$, with a removable singularity at $\theta = 0$,
  computed below a cutoff from its series $-\theta^2/12 - \theta^4/1440 + O(\theta^6)$
  [](eq:math/chart-volume/so3-exp), in a kernel or in a composite with its closed form guarded, as
  the core says of $R$. Its order is the type's to claim: a term over the type has Hessians only
  if $\Lambda$ admits Hessian passes.

**The acceptance-test sphere.** The core's acceptance test declares the unit sphere $S^2 \subset
\R^3$ outside the core, with the stereographic retraction and inverse [](eq:archive/core/math/sphere)
and a basis $B(\point)$ orthonormal on the sphere. Its normalized measure is surface measure
$\sigma$, since $\det(B\transpose B) = 1$, and its chart is global and full: the retraction is
defined for every $\tangent$, $Q$ inverts it on all of $\R^2$ by the computation that accompanies
[](eq:archive/core/math/sphere), and it reaches every point but $-\mu$. Its log-volume is that of
[the note's table](../../math/chart-volume/#the-usual-charts),

$$
	\Lambda_{S^2}(\mu, \tangent) = -2 \log\bigl(1 + \tfrac14 \lVert \tangent \rVert^2\bigr), @tag(sphere-volume)
$$

independent of $\mu$, smooth everywhere as written, and a composite of the core's primitives. Read
at the coordinate of a point, it has a form in storage too: with $c = \mu\transpose \point$,
$\lVert B(\mu)\transpose \point \rVert^2 = 1 - c^2$, so $\lVert \point \ominus \mu \rVert^2 / 4 =
(1 - c)/(1 + c)$ and $\Lambda_{S^2}(\mu, \point \ominus \mu) = 2 \log\bigl((1 + \mu\transpose
\point) / 2\bigr)$. The sphere is the natural type to test a declaration on: it is curved, already
written against the core's public interface alone, and its volume is known in closed form.

## Declared, not computed

The volume of an embedded type's chart could be computed from its retraction alone.
[%full](statement:math/chart-volume/normalized) gives it, and it extends to every type the core has.

<Statement {...area} />

<Proof>

The first part of the proof of [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/normalized) gives the volume of
the chart with respect to $\sigma$ as $\det(\partial_2 R\transpose \partial_2 R)^{1/2}$, and with
respect to $h \, \dd \sigma$ it is that times $h(R(\mu, \tangent))$. On the core's Euclidean and
positive types, stored as $\R^n$ and its open subset $\positive^n$, the measure the storage induces
is Lebesgue measure, so $h = 1$. On an embedded type, $h$ is that of
[%full](statement:math/chart-volume/normalized). A product is stored as its factors' storage
concatenated, the measure that storage induces is the product of the factors', because the
coordinates of different factors are orthogonal, and its reference measure is the product
$\prod_i h_i \, \dd \sigma_i$. For the second part, take $g = 1$ in
[](eq:math/chart-volume/image): $\int_{U_\mu} v_\mu = \nu(V_\mu)$, which is $\nu(\manifold)$ when
the chart is full.

</Proof>

The core does not compute the volume this way. In a term, the log-volume is a value of the forward
pass, its gradient enters the term's gradient, and its Hessian the term's Hessian. Computed by the
area formula, the forward value would contain $\partial_2 R$, a first derivative of the retraction,
so its gradient would need the second derivatives of $R$ and its Hessian the third, and the core's
operations have derivative maps of second order at most. Nor can a composite be built from a pass: it is
built from the values of operations, and a derivative map is what a pass over it computes, not a
value in the compilation graph. And every evaluation would cost $\dimension$ tangent passes
over $R$, a Gram matrix and its log-determinant, where the declared formulas of the
[note's table](../../math/chart-volume/#the-usual-charts) are one scalar function of the tangent
vector: $2$, $4$ and $6 \log \operatorname{sinc}(\theta / 2)$ for the exponential charts of $SO(3)$,
$SE(3)$ and $SE_2(3)$, and $-2 \log(1 + \lVert \tangent \rVert^2 / 4)$ for the Cayley chart of the
rotations and the stereographic chart of the sphere.

A type therefore declares its log-volume, and [%full](statement:area) is what a test checks the
declaration against. Its right side needs only first derivatives, which a tangent pass over $R$
gives, $\dimension$ columns at its second input, and the bases $B$ at the moved point, a second
tangent pass at $\tangent = 0$:

$$
	\Lambda(\mu, \tangent) = \tfrac12 \log \det\bigl(\partial_2 R(\mu, \tangent)\transpose\, \partial_2 R(\mu, \tangent)\bigr)
	- \tfrac12 \log \det\bigl(B(\otherPoint)\transpose B(\otherPoint)\bigr), \qquad \otherPoint = R(\mu, \tangent), @tag(area-check)
$$

at points $\mu$ of the type and tangent vectors $\tangent$ of its domain, near $0$ and far from it.
Two smooth functions that agree on an open set agree in every derivative there, so a declaration
that passes this check at enough points has the right derivatives as well, and the derivative maps
of $\Lambda$ itself are checked as any operation's are. Three further identities are checked with
it, each cheap and each catching a different mistake:

1. $\Lambda(\mu, 0) = 0$ at every $\mu$, the normalization, which a log-volume computed against
   surface measure rather than the normalized measure fails by $\tfrac12 \log \det(B\transpose B)$;
2. $Q(\mu, R(\mu, \tangent)) = \tangent$ for $\tangent$ throughout the domain, not only near $0$,
   which is the inverse on the whole chart that the definition asks for; and, where the domain is
   not global, that $\Omega$ is true at the points of the check and false beyond them;
3. on a compact type with a full chart, the total mass of [%full](statement:area),

$$
	\int_{U_\mu} e^{\Lambda(\mu, \tangent)} \, \dd \tangent = \nu(\manifold), @tag(total-mass)
$$

   the same at every $\mu$, which a chart that is not full, or a log-volume wrong by a constant,
   fails. For the sphere with [](eq:sphere-volume) it is $4 \pi$, for the rotations with either
   the exponential or the Cayley chart $8 \pi^2$, the same for both because both have the
   left-translated bases and so normalize the same measure.

The check [](eq:area-check) holds on the core's own types too, with the second term absent: on
$\positive^n$ its first term is $\sum_i \log(\mu_i e^{\tangent_i})$, which is
[](eq:core-volumes). On a product it is the sum of the factors' checks, since $\partial_2 R$ is
block diagonal and the second term has a term for each embedded factor alone.

## Products

A product of continuous types has the product of its factors' reference measures, and by
[%full](statement:math/chart-volume/product) its chart's log-volume is the sum of its factors'
over the stacked tangent: with $\mu = (\mu_1, \dots, \mu_k)$ stored as its factors' storage
concatenated and $\tangent = (\tangent_1; \dots; \tangent_k)$,

$$
	\Lambda(\mu, \tangent) = \sum_{i=1}^k \Lambda_i(\mu_i, \tangent_i), @tag(product-volume)
$$

each term read from the slices of $\mu$ and $\tangent$ that belong to its factor. The product's
chart is global if every factor's is, and its domain is otherwise the product of the factors',
whose discretization is the conjunction of theirs, a global factor's being always true; it is full
because every factor's is. A product has a volume exactly when every factor has one. The core's own
types always do, so a product of them always does, and a product with an embedded factor that
declares none has none. On the core's runtime products, whose factors and dimensions are known only
when the quantity is created, [](eq:product-volume) is assembled then, as the product's retraction
is.

The product's measure is the product of its factors', and not, in general, the measure the product
retraction normalizes: a positive factor contributes Lebesgue measure. By
[%full](statement:math/chart-volume/product) the two agree when every factor's measure is its own
normalized one, which is the case for products of embedded and Euclidean types.

## Densities against the reference measures

With a reference measure on every continuous type that has a volume, the appendix's account of a
model's probability space reaches the curved types without change, and what the core states
against base measures is stated against these.

**Densities through a chart.** A distribution that draws a point as $\mu \oplus \varepsilon$, with
$\varepsilon$ of density $f$ on $\R^\dimension$ and in the domain almost surely, has with respect to
the type's reference measure the log-density

$$
	\log \density(\point) = \log f(\point \ominus \mu) - \Lambda\bigl(\iota(\mu), \point \ominus \mu\bigr), @tag(chart-density)
$$

by [%full](statement:math/chart-volume/pushforward) and [](eq:declared), on every type, the core's
included: on $\R^n$ the correction vanishes, and on $\positive^n$ it is
[](eq:archive/core/math/log-chart-density). The same holds of a sampler's log-density
$\log \proposal_t$, and of a parameter's proposal. This is the general form of the one place the
core names where base measures are easy to mismatch, a density written in a chart, and the
importance weight [](eq:archive/core/math/weight) is right on a curved type when its terms and its
proposals keep to it.

**Importance weights.** [%full](statement:archive/core/math/importance) takes $\dd \point_F$ to be
the product of the free quantities' base measures. A free quantity's base measure is now its type's
reference measure: Lebesgue measure on the Euclidean and positive types, the measure its retraction
normalizes on an embedded type, counting measure on a discrete type, and the product of its
factors' on a product.

**Retirement.** The integral of [%full](statement:archive/core/math/retirement) over the retired
free quantities $E_F$ is against the product of their reference measures. The core does not compute
it, but the replacement it checks stands for it, and whoever approximates it in a chart works with
[](eq:math/chart-volume/image): for a quantity of $E_F$ whose chart at $\hat \point$ is full,

$$
	\int_\manifold e^{\ell(\point)} \, \dd \nu(\point)
	= \int_{U_{\hat \point}} e^{\ell(\hat \point \oplus \tangent) + \lambda_{\hat \point}(\tangent)} \, \dd \tangent, @tag(chart-integral)
$$

so a Laplace approximation in the chart expands $\ell(\hat \point \oplus \tangent) +
\lambda_{\hat \point}(\tangent)$, not $\ell(\hat \point \oplus \tangent)$ alone. On an embedded
type $\lambda_{\hat \point}(0) = 0$ and the log-volume enters through its derivatives only, through
its Hessian when its gradient at $0$ vanishes, as for every chart of the note's table:
$-\tfrac16 I$ on the exponential chart of the rotations, $-I$ on the Cayley and stereographic
charts. On a positive quantity it enters through its value $\sum_i \log \hat \point_i$ and its
gradient, the vector of ones.

## Types without a volume

An embedded type that declares no volume has no reference measure, and so no densities: there is no
measure against which a function of its points could be the density of a law. The appendix
therefore holds for the types that have a volume, and of a quantity of a type that has none, the
core asks what needs no measure on it and refuses what does.

1. **No term scores it.** A term that scores a quantity is that quantity's conditional density
   ([the appendix](../core/math.svx#appendix-the-probability-space-of-a-model)), so the core
   refuses a term that scores a quantity of a type without a volume. Such a quantity is always a
   parameter.
2. **It has no proposal.** A proposal is a density with respect to the parameter's base measure,
   and the core refuses one for a parameter of a type without a volume. A query that leaves such a
   parameter free can therefore not be generated, which the core already refuses, naming a free
   quantity that has neither a sampler nor a proposal.
3. **It is not integrated out.** A retirement that would integrate such a quantity out, with it in
   $E_F$, is refused: the integral of [%full](statement:archive/core/math/retirement) is not
   defined. It may be retired with a given value, in $E_O$, since fixing a quantity needs no
   measure on it.

Everything else is allowed. A term may be conditioned on such a quantity, since a conditional
density of another quantity given it needs no measure on it; a query may observe it; and a query
may leave it free, as optimization does: its log-density is then a function of the quantity, whose
maximum is a likelihood estimate, but not a density of it, so an algorithm that integrates over the
query's free quantities, a sampler or an evidence, must refuse it. A distribution on a type without
a volume can therefore only be a *likelihood factor over data*: with the measured value a constant
$z$, a function such as $-\tfrac12 \lVert W(z \ominus h(\point)) \rVert^2$ of the state scores
nothing and needs no measure. That is how factor-graph libraries hold every measurement, and it
remains available on every type; a volume is what makes the measurement a quantity of its own,
with a law that can be sampled and integrated.

## In skoro's terms

- **Every continuous type has, or declares, a reference measure and a chart log-volume**
  $\Lambda(\mu, \tangent)$, an operation on the storage of the base point and the tangent vector.
  The Euclidean types (real, vector, matrix) have Lebesgue measure and $\Lambda = 0$; the positive
  orthant has Lebesgue measure and $\Lambda = \sum_i (\log \mu_i + \tangent_i)$ [](eq:core-volumes),
  which is $\sum_i \log \point_i$ at the coordinate of $\point$; both charts are global and full.
  Discrete types have counting measure and no chart.
- **An embedded type may declare its chart's volume**, by [%full](statement:declaration): a domain,
  global or given by a discretization of $(\mu, \tangent)$, on which the chart is a diffeomorphism
  onto a full image and inverted by the type's $Q$, and a log-volume $\Lambda$, an operation on
  Euclidean values with derivative maps to the type's order, smooth as computed at $\tangent = 0$.
  Its reference measure is then the one its retraction normalizes, the same for every storage with
  the same bases: Haar measure on the groups, surface measure on the sphere.
- **A product's log-volume** is the sum of its factors' over the stacked tangent
  [](eq:product-volume), its measure the product of theirs; it has a volume when every factor has.
- **The log-volume is differentiated** with respect to the tangent vector and, through the
  inclusion, to the base point in its chart, [](eq:declared), whenever a term contains it, as the
  densities of [](eq:chart-density) do; its order bounds theirs.
- **A declaration is tested**, not trusted: against the area formula [](eq:area-check), computed by
  first-order passes over the retraction, against $\Lambda(\mu, 0) = 0$, against the inverse on the
  whole domain, and, on a compact type, against the total mass [](eq:total-mass). The core's
  acceptance-test sphere declares [](eq:sphere-volume) and is checked so.
- **The appendix** gives every quantity of a type with a volume its reference measure, against
  which terms, samplers, proposals, importance weights and retirements are stated. A quantity of a
  type without one is never scored, has no proposal and is never integrated out by a retirement; it
  is a parameter, observed or free, and a distribution on its type is a likelihood factor over data.

## Open questions

> [!TODO] Charts that are not full
> [%full](statement:declaration) asks that a declared chart be full, so that a density read through
> $Q$ is right at every point a model can be given. That excludes, from declaring a volume, a type
> whose retraction is the normalization chart of the sphere (a hemisphere) or the polar chart of the
> rotations (the rotations by less than a quarter turn), though the laws those charts push forward
> are proper laws, supported on the image. The alternative admits them with a second
> discretization, true on the image $V_\mu$ ($\mu\transpose \point > 0$ for the normalization chart,
> $\operatorname{tr}(\mu\transpose \point) > 1$ for the polar), at which a term selects a
